### [QUIC-LB Update](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-quic-quic-lb-update-00)
- **Presenter**: Martin Duke
- **Document**: [`draft-ietf-quic-load-balancers`](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-quic-load-balancers/)
- **Technical Overview**:
  - QUIC-LB provides a structured connection ID (CID) specification allowing layer-4 load balancers to route packets to designated backend servers without terminating connections.
  - CIDs feature a Config ID (3 bits), length self-encoding (5 bits), Server ID, Nonce, and extra server-use bytes.
  - Three encryption modes are defined: Plaintext, single-block AES-ECB (for 16-byte Server ID + Nonce), and the 4-pass Feistel cipher (Four-pass encoding) for variable-length encrypted CIDs.
  - Deployments: Plaintext support is deployed on AWS NLB and Envoy; encrypted QUIC-LB is currently being deployed by Google and another major vendor.
- **Discussion**:
  - Christian Huitema noted that 4-pass Feistel evaluation performs comparably to 64-bit ciphers like Blowfish.
  - Per Sundström recommended placing YANG modules in normative text and requesting an early YANG Doctors review alongside IANA registrations.
  - Antonio Gurtov asked about hardware load balancer vendor interest and motivations.
  - Chairs proposed proceeding with Working Group Last Call (WGLC) alongside an early security review. The separate, expired retry offload draft will be formally retired/abandoned due to lack of implementation interest.

